---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
   { id: 'session', label: 'Start a screen reader' },
   { id: 'methods', label: 'Move, type, and read' },
   { id: 'checks', label: 'Assertions' },
   { id: 'example', label: 'Example test file' },
   { id: 'real', label: 'VoiceOver and NVDA' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Write screen reader tests"
   summary="Start a screen reader from a test, move through the page one element at a time, and assert on what the screen reader announced. a11ied/test depends on no test runner and works under Vitest, Jest, Mocha, Playwright Test, or a script. a11ied/vitest and a11ied/browser have their own guide."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="install">
      <h2>Install</h2>
      <pre><code>npm install --save-dev a11ied</code></pre>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied/test</code><span><code>screenReader()</code>, <code>queuedScreenReader()</code>, and the <code>ScreenReader</code> class. It depends on no test runner and works under Vitest, Jest, Mocha, Playwright Test, or a script.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied/vitest</code><span>The same, plus <code>expect</code> matchers and a <code>test</code> whose <code>sr</code> fixture is a queued screen reader. See <a href="/guides/vitest">Test with Vitest</a>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a11ied/browser</code><span>The same again, for a component the test mounted in Vitest browser mode. See <a href="/guides/vitest">Test with Vitest</a>.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         A test that opens a URL runs the simulated screen reader inside a headless Chromium page,
         so the page's scripts run. Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium on the machine is used first, and
         <code>npx playwright install chromium</code> adds one when none is installed. Inline
         HTML runs in jsdom and needs no browser.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="session">
      <h2>Start a screen reader</h2>
      <pre><code>import {'{'} screenReader {'}'} from 'a11ied/test';

await using sr = await screenReader({'{'} url: 'http://localhost:3000/checkout' {'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>screenReader</code> takes <code>sr</code> (<code>virtual</code> by default,
         <code>voiceover</code>, or <code>nvda</code>), a <code>url</code> or inline
         <code>html</code>, and <code>mode</code>. The default mode, <code>in-process</code>, runs
         the screen reader inside the test process and never touches the per-user state directory, so
         tests in parallel workers do not compete for the one active <code>a1 sr</code> session.
         <code>mode: 'broker'</code> starts the detached process that <code>a1 sr</code> shares
         instead, so <code>a1 sr transcript</code> in another terminal reads the same session.
      </p>
      <p>
         The <code>ScreenReader</code> object implements <code>Symbol.asyncDispose</code>. With <code>await using</code>, the
         session stops when the block ends, whether the test passed or threw. A runner without
         explicit resource management calls <code>await sr.stop()</code> in its cleanup hook
         instead. Every call after a stop throws <code>session-stopped</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr.session</code> reports what you got: <code>sr</code>, <code>mode</code>,
         <code>url</code>, and for the simulated screen reader the <code>engine</code>, which is
         <code>browser</code> for a Playwright page and <code>jsdom</code> for inline HTML or a
         machine with no Chromium.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>queuedScreenReader</code> takes the same options and returns a
         <code>QueuedScreenReader</code>. One <code>await</code> starts it. After that a method
         call queues a command and returns at once, and the queued commands run one at a time in
         the order they were queued. Await a call only when the test needs its value.
      </p>
      <pre><code>import {'{'} queuedScreenReader {'}'} from 'a11ied/test';

await using sr = await queuedScreenReader({'{'} url: 'http://localhost:3000/checkout' {'}'});

sr.goTo({'{'} role: 'button', name: 'Pay' {'}'});
sr.activate();
sr.expectSpoken(/order placed/iu);

const item = await sr.read();</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>await using</code> calls <code>sr.stop()</code>, which runs every queued command,
         stops the reader even when one of them threw, and then rethrows that error.
         <code>sr.drain()</code> waits for the queued commands without stopping the reader.
         <code>sr.session</code> is a plain property on a queued reader and needs no
         <code>await</code>. <code>sr.reader</code> is the underlying <code>ScreenReader</code>,
         for a call that has to run before the queue empties.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="methods">
      <h2>Move, type, and read</h2>
      <p>
         The methods mirror the <code>sr</code> subcommands. A screen reader moves through a page
         one element at a time: a heading, a link, a button, a form field, a paragraph, a
         landmark (a section such as <code>main</code> or <code>navigation</code>). Every method
         that moves the cursor or sends input returns the announcement, the text the screen
         reader spoke when it reached the element.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>next(kind?, options?)</code>, <code>previous(kind?, options?)</code></div><span>The next or previous element, or a jump by <code>heading</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>landmark</code>, <code>control</code>, <code>button</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>graphic</code>, <code>region</code>, or <code>form-field</code>. Options: <code>level</code> for headings, <code>times</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>top()</code>, <code>bottom()</code>, <code>interact()</code>, <code>stopInteracting()</code>, <code>activate()</code>, <code>escape()</code></div><span>The portable verbs.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>press(...chords)</code>, <code>type(text)</code></div><span><code>press('Tab', 'Enter')</code> sends two chords in order.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>read()</code><span>The element under the cursor: <code>role</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>states</code>, <code>level</code>, <code>phrase</code>, and <code>source</code>, which says where each field came from.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>title()</code><span>The page title, or the window summary on VoiceOver and the window title on NVDA.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>find(text)</code>, <code>goTo({'{'} role, name {'}'})</code></div><span>Move the cursor to the next element whose text contains <code>text</code>, or forward until the current element has the role, the name, or both. Both throw when nothing matches.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>elements(kind)</code>, <code>readAll()</code>, <code>walk()</code></div><span>Every element of one kind from the top of the page, every element from the cursor to the end, and the same from the top. Each returns the elements announced, capped by <code>max</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>table(move)</code><span><code>next-cell</code> and the other moves return the announcement. <code>row-header</code> and <code>column-header</code> return the header text.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>open(url)</code>, <code>open({'{'} html {'}'})</code></div><span>Another page in the same session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>checkpoint(label)</code>, <code>transcript({'{'} since, tail {'}'})</code></div><span>Mark a point, and read the timestamped entries since it.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wait({'{'} for, timeoutMs {'}'})</code><span>Polls the transcript for an announcement made after the call and returns it, or throws at the timeout, 5000 ms by default. <code>wait({'{'} ms {'}'})</code> pauses.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>perform(command)</code>, <code>screenshot(path)</code>, <code>focus()</code></div><span>A named command from <code>a1 sr list</code>, a VoiceOver cursor screenshot, and a refocus of the session's window.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         A <code>QueuedScreenReader</code> has every one of these methods, with the same
         parameters. Each returns a thenable instead of a promise, and the command runs after
         the ones queued before it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="checks">
      <h2>Assertions</h2>
      <pre><code>await sr.checkpoint('opened');
await sr.activate();
await sr.expectSpoken('dialog, Preferences', {'{'} since: 'opened' {'}'});
await sr.expectSpoken(/error/iu, {'{'} not: true {'}'});
await sr.expectOn({'{'} role: 'textbox', name: 'Nickname' {'}'});
await sr.expectSpokenInOrder(['dialog, Preferences', 'textbox, Nickname'], {'{'} since: 'opened' {'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>expectSpoken</code> takes text, matched without regard to case, or a
         <code>RegExp</code>. <code>since</code> searches only the announcements after that
         checkpoint, and <code>not</code> passes when nothing matches. On failure it throws a
         <code>ScreenReaderAssertionError</code> whose message says what was expected, how many
         announcements were searched, and what the screen reader said:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>ScreenReaderAssertionError: "button, Pay now" was not announced in the transcript (3 phrases checked).
The reader said:
  "document"
  "heading, Sign up, level 1"
  "button, Create account"</code></pre>
      <p>
         The error has <code>exitCode: 4</code>, the CLI's assertion code, plus
         <code>expected</code> and <code>phrases</code>. A script can set
         <code>process.exitCode</code> from it.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>expectOn({'{'} role, name {'}'})</code> throws when the element under the cursor does not have
         the role or the name given. The message says which element the cursor is on, the same
         text the <code>toBeOn</code> matcher prints. <code>expectSpokenInOrder([a, b, c], {'{'} since {'}'})</code>
         throws when a match is not found after the previous one, with the same text the
         <code>toHaveSpokenInOrder</code> matcher prints. Both throw
         <code>ScreenReaderAssertionError</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="example">
      <h2>Example test file</h2>
      <p>
         The page is a combobox labeled Select country with three options, Canada first. Arrow
         Down from the collapsed combobox expands it and moves to the first option. The dev
         server runs on port 3000.
      </p>
      <pre><code>import {'{'} queuedScreenReader {'}'} from 'a11ied/test';

it('announces active option and expanded state', async () => {'{'}
   await using sr = await queuedScreenReader({'{'}
      url: 'http://localhost:3000/components/combobox',
   {'}'});

   sr.press('Tab');
   sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country, collapsed');

   sr.press('ArrowDown');
   sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country, expanded');
   sr.expectSpoken('option, Canada, 1 of 3');
{'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         Under Vitest the same test drops <code>queuedScreenReader</code> and
         <code>await using</code> for the <code>sr</code> fixture. See
         <a href="/guides/vitest">Test with Vitest</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="real">
      <h2>VoiceOver and NVDA</h2>
      <p>
         <code>screenReader({'{'} sr: 'voiceover', url {'}'})</code> opens the URL in the system browser,
         waits for that window to come to the front, starts VoiceOver, and refocuses the window,
         the same steps <code>a1 sr start</code> takes. VoiceOver needs the one-time
         <code>a1 setup</code> on the machine, takes over the screen while the test runs, and
         reads whatever is in front, so run these tests one at a time and never in a worker pool.
         Prefer <code>mode: 'broker'</code> there: a session that outlives a crashed test can
         be stopped with <code>a1 sr stop</code>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
